      id: "section-8", title: "Major East-flowing Rivers",
      content: "\n<h3>Major East-flowing Rivers</h3>\n<p>The Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri generally enter the Bay of Bengal. Their lower courses can form deltas through sediment deposition. Delta regions are agriculturally productive but vulnerable to flooding, cyclones, erosion and salinity. The Godavari has the largest Peninsular river basin in India by area.</p>\n<h4>How to understand this topic</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start with location:</strong> identify the region, feature or process on the map and note its relationship with neighbouring areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify controls:</strong> consider relief, climate, water, geology, distance, accessibility, population or human decisions as appropriate.</li>\n<li><strong>Observe spatial variation:</strong> ask why the feature is stronger, weaker, denser, wetter, drier, higher or lower in different places.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain the process:</strong> connect cause, process and visible geographical result instead of memorising disconnected facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Connect with people and environment:</strong> explain effects on agriculture, settlement, transport, economy, hazards, ecosystems or conservation where relevant.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Geographical significance</h4>\n<p>Major East-flowing Rivers is important because geography studies spatial patterns and relationships between people and the physical environment. A strong answer should normally contain a definition or opening idea, location or setting, major characteristics, reasons or controlling factors, and significance. If a visual is provided, use it as evidence for the written explanation.</p>\n<h4>Observation points</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Read the title, legend and labels before interpreting a map or figure.</li>\n<li>Look for concentration, contrast, direction, distribution and regional variation.</li>\n<li>Distinguish a physical cause from a human consequence.</li>\n<li>Use exact geographical vocabulary rather than vague words such as 'many', 'big' or 'different'.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Answer-writing focus</h4>\n<p>For school answers, write in a logical sequence. Define the key idea first, then explain the main characteristics and reasons. Use named examples when they clarify the concept. Avoid presenting a regional pattern as if it applies equally everywhere in India.</p>\n\n<div class=\"geography-visual\" style=\"margin:20px 0;padding:14px;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid #ddd;background:#fafafa\"><div style=\"font-weight:700\">MAP: India rivers map</div><img src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/India-physical-map.png\" alt=\"India rivers map\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"display:block;max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:10px auto;border-radius:10px\"><div style=\"font-size:.88em\"><strong>Source/Credit:</strong> Nichalp, CC BY-SA 3.0 / GFDL. <strong>Use:</strong> study the labelled locations/patterns and connect them to the surrounding explanation.</div></div>\n<div class=\"deep-geography-note\"><h4>🔎 Deeper geographical reading</h4><p>This section should be studied as a connected geographical explanation rather than as isolated facts. First identify the location or setting. Next describe the main observable characteristics. Then explain the physical or human factors that produce the pattern. Finally connect the pattern with its effects on people, resources, ecosystems, transport, agriculture, hazards or regional development, depending on the topic.</p><p><strong>Map/figure method:</strong> identify the title, orientation, labels, legend and scale where available. Locate the feature, compare neighbouring regions, describe the distribution, and then explain why that distribution exists. A map is evidence: use it to support the written answer rather than merely memorising its appearance.</p><p><strong>Revision chain:</strong> location → characteristics → controlling factors → process → spatial variation → significance → human/environmental relationship. This chain can be converted into a 3-mark, 5-mark or long-answer response without losing the geographical reasoning.</p></div>",
