  keyTerms: [{"term": "Latitude", "meaning": "Angular distance north or south of the Equator."}, {"term": "Longitude", "meaning": "Angular distance east or west of the Prime Meridian."}, {"term": "Physiography", "meaning": "The study and description of major physical landform divisions."}, {"term": "Drainage Basin", "meaning": "The area drained by a river and its tributaries."}, {"term": "Water Divide", "meaning": "An upland separating neighbouring drainage basins."}, {"term": "Monsoon", "meaning": "A seasonal wind system associated with a marked seasonal reversal in wind direction."}, {"term": "Natural Vegetation", "meaning": "Plant communities that have developed naturally under local environmental conditions."}, {"term": "Biodiversity", "meaning": "The variety of living organisms and ecosystems in an area."}, {"term": "Population Density", "meaning": "The number of people living per unit area."}, {"term": "Migration", "meaning": "Movement of people from one place to another with a change or intended change of residence."}, {"term": "Tributary", "meaning": "A smaller stream or river that joins a main river."}, {"term": "Meander", "meaning": "A bend or curve in a river channel."}, {"term": "Estuary", "meaning": "A tidal river mouth where fresh and salt water mix."}],
  chapterSummary: "Drainage refers to the river system of an area. A drainage basin is the area drained by a river and its tributaries, while a water divide separates neighbouring basins. India's rivers are linked with relief, climate, agriculture, settlements, irrigation, hydropower, ecosystems and cultural life. A river system contains the main river and its tributaries. A drainage basin is the area drained by that network and a water divide is an upland separating two basins. Drainage patterns are influenced by slope, rock structure, geological history and relief. Rivers erode, transport and deposit sediments, create valleys and floodplains, recharge water systems and support ecosystems. Indian drainage is broadly divided into Himalayan and Peninsular systems. Himalayan rivers are generally perennial because their water comes from rainfall as well as snow and glacier melt. Many have long courses and large alluvial basins. Peninsular rivers generally have more established courses and are often strongly seasonal because their flow depends heavily on the monsoon. The Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems are the major Himalayan drainage systems discussed at school level. They originate in or near the Himalayan region and cross mountains, plains and lowlands. High sediment loads, seasonal discharge changes and active landscape processes help explain their floodplains, channels and depositional features. The Indus rises in the Tibetan region and flows through the northwestern subcontinent before reaching the Arabian Sea. Important tributaries include the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej. The basin is heavily dependent on irrigation because parts of the region receive limited rainfall. Since the river crosses international boundaries, water sharing is a major example of transboundary river geography. The Ganga system receives many Himalayan and Peninsular tributaries, including the Yamuna, Ghaghara, Gandak and Kosi. Its basin supports dense settlement, agriculture, cities, industries and cultural activities. These uses also produce pressures from sewage, industrial pollution, solid waste, extraction and habitat change. The lower basin connects with the large deltaic environment of the Ganga-Brahmaputra system. The Brahmaputra originates in the Tibetan region and enters India through the eastern Himalayas. In Assam it forms a broad river valley and receives several tributaries. High sediment loads and strong seasonal changes in discharge contribute to frequent flooding and channel changes. Floodplains and wetlands are ecologically valuable, so flood management must consider both safety and ecosystem functions. Peninsular rivers are generally older in their landscape setting. The Narmada and Tapi flow westward, while the Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri generally flow eastward. Many are strongly seasonal and have been regulated through reservoirs and irrigation projects. Such projects provide water and power but can also alter sediment movement, fisheries and downstream ecosystems. The Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri generally enter the Bay of Bengal. Their lower courses can form deltas through sediment deposition. Delta regions are agriculturally productive but vulnerable to flooding, cyclones, erosion and salinity. The Godavari has the largest Peninsular river basin in India by area. The Narmada and Tapi flow into the Arabian Sea and are associated with structural valleys. They do not build the very large deltas typical of many east-flowing rivers. The Narmada flows through a rift-related valley and is associated with gorges and waterfalls in parts of its course. Lakes store water and can be formed by tectonic, glacial, river, coastal or human processes. Wular, Dal, Chilika and Sambhar illustrate different lake environments. Lakes support fisheries, biodiversity, tourism and water storage, but pollution, encroachment and altered inflows can damage them. Rivers support irrigation, drinking water, industries, fisheries, hydropower and ecosystems. Many settlements developed along rivers because water is essential for life and agriculture. Rivers also have deep cultural significance, making conservation an environmental, economic and social responsibility. River pollution may come from untreated sewage, industrial effluent, agricultural runoff, solid waste and other human activities. Conservation requires wastewater treatment, pollution control, protection of wetlands and floodplains, efficient water use, monitoring, community participation and maintenance of environmental flows. River restoration is a basin-wide task, not merely removal of visible waste.",
